<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1513–1571</span> · Also known as Hans of Küstrin, John of Küstrin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John, Margrave of Brandenburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kostrzyn-nad-odra">Küstrin</a>, known as Hans of Küstrin, was a younger son of Elector Joachim I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> who received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neumark">Neumark</a> east of the Oder in 1535. He introduced the Lutheran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in 1538, turned Küstrin into a strong fortress and ran a thrifty, efficient government. He died without sons, and his lands returned to Brandenburg.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-margrave-of-brandenburg-kustrin.jpg" alt="John, Margrave of Brandenburg-Küstrin" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unidentified painter · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JohannBrandenburgK%C3%BCstrin.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
